  "salt": "HtL17wznhV-Z9AwhlhGR8Q==",
  "text": "The Celts set the oak above the other wild trees. Its nut crop fed the swine and in time the farm table. The trunk became beams and the peel filled the tan pit for leather. Pliny the Elder wrote how the druid cut the mistletoe from the oak's limb. Sacred groves in Gaul and the wooden frames of the dead from the Hallstatt and La Tène age placed the oak close to what comes after life. That dark realm may have known a god, yet the written word begins only with Rome. Folk belief held that the felled tree could wail at the axe, and that the fey dwelled within old trunks. The name druid rises from an older term for this wood, a trace of its old awe in speech.",
